<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bruno the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">925–<a href="/years/965">965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Bruno I of Cologne, Bruno I, Archbishop of Cologne · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bruno the Great (925–965) was Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> from 953 and a younger brother of Otto I. Otto also made him Duke of Lotharingia, and around 959 he divided the duchy into Upper and Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, each under a subordinate duke. A learned churchman, he was a central figure of the Ottonian system of governing the kingdom through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bruno-the-great.jpg" alt="Bruno the Great" width="200" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bruno_the_Great.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their position was precarious: in 958 Archbishop <b>Bruno of Cologne</b>, ruling Lotharingia for his brother Otto I, exiled Reginar III, and Hainaut was divided into the counties of Mons and Valenciennes under royal appointees.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 959 Archbishop <b>Bruno of Cologne</b>, brother of Otto I and duke of Lotharingia, divided it into Lower Lorraine in the north and Upper Lorraine along the Moselle and upper Meuse, entrusting the south to Frederick, count of Bar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Bruno</b> led the Saxons against the Vikings and was killed fighting them in 880, and his younger son Otto the Illustrious held a dominant position in Saxony and Thuringia until his death in 912.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Otto I then gave the duchy to his brother <b>Bruno</b>, archbishop of Cologne, who divided it in 959 into the Duchy of Upper Lorraine and the Duchy of Lower Lorraine.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-lotharingia">Duchy of Lotharingia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 959 Archbishop <b>Bruno</b> of Cologne divided the Duchy of Lotharingia, creating a separate duchy for the lands of the lower Meuse, Scheldt and Rhine.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Duchy of Lower Lorraine</a></li><li><blockquote>In 953 Otto I made his brother <b>Bruno</b> archbishop and also duke of Lotharingia, founding the temporal power of the see.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-cologne">Electorate of Cologne</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Duchy of Saxony. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.27,4.03&amp;year=951" aria-label="Reims on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4643 446 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Bruno the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5093 716h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5093 716h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=49.27,4.03&amp;year=951">What was here in 951 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
